From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 19:23:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11335/11362] Close two open items from 22:15Z with their
 causes

The 83-minute quiet was one task hitting a hard wall-clock cap at 4803s, not a
stall; the retry succeeded 23 minutes later. The 22:15Z entry offered two readings
and the truth was a third I did not enumerate: one task consuming the whole window
and being killed by its own cap, distinguishable from both because it leaves a
FAIL row rather than silence. No new memory entry -- the general form is already
recorded as "a supervisor asleep above a busy worker looks like a hang".

Recording what the base rate bought, since the discipline usually shows up as an
absence of wrong reports rather than as a result: because the day's gaps were
written down, an 83-minute window read as the top of an existing range instead of
as degradation, and nothing was escalated for something that cleared in 23 minutes.

The orphaned xdist workers are gone and GPU 0 is back to 4 MiB. The owner stays
UNIDENTIFIED and is not retro-fitted now that the problem cleared: a process that
exits on its own says nothing about who started it, and the three candidates are as
plausible as they were. The temptation once something clears is to close it with
whichever story fits.

